  "worked_solution": "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 1-t & <0 \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (1,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
